1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,180
Here‘s a staggering statistic.
这里有一个惊人的统计数据。

2
00:00:15,180 --> 00:00:22,420
Scientists have calculated the total amount of plastic ever made: 8.3 billion tonnes.
科学家们计算出了有史以来生产的塑料总量：83亿吨。

3
00:00:22,420 --> 00:00:29,360
Looked at another way, that’s as heavy as 25,000 Empire State Buildings or one billion elephants.
换个角度看，这相当于25000座帝国大厦的重量，或者10亿头大象的重量。

4
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:34,670
And incredibly, almost all of it has been made in the last 65 years.
令人难以置信的是，几乎所有这些塑料都是在过去65年里生产出来的。

5
00:00:34,670 --> 00:00:36,530
So what‘s the problem?
那么问题出在哪里呢？

6
00:00:36,530 --> 00:00:41,860
Much plastic is in the form of packaging which is used just once and then thrown away.
很多塑料是以包装的形式存在的，这些包装只使用一次就被扔掉了。

7
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:52,310
According to a major new study from the University of California, 9% of this is recycled, 12% is incinerated and 79% goes to landfill.
根据加利福尼亚大学的一项重要新研究，其中9%的塑料被回收利用，12%被焚烧，79%被填埋到垃圾填埋场。

8
00:00:52,310 --> 00:00:57,750
And because most plastic doesn’t biodegrade, once it‘s in the ground, it stays there.
而且由于大多数塑料不会生物降解，一旦进入地下，就会一直留在那里。

9
00:00:57,750 --> 00:01:06,440
It’s a situation that has led the paper‘s lead author, ecologist Dr Roland Geyer, to say that we are “rapidly heading towards ’Planet Plastic‘”.
这种情况使得该论文的第一作者、生态学家罗兰·盖耶博士表示，我们正“迅速走向‘塑料星球’”。

10
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:11,420
He believes that there’s already enough waste out there to cover the whole of Argentina.
他认为，现在已经有足够多的塑料垃圾可以覆盖整个阿根廷了。

11
00:01:11,420 --> 00:01:19,040
The team behind this report also estimate that eight million tonnes of plastic waste are escaping into the sea every year.
同时，这份报告背后的研究团队预估，每年有800万吨塑料垃圾流入海洋。

12
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:26,800
This has generated concern that plastic is entering the food chain, through fish and other sea life which ingest the smaller fragments.
这引发了人们的担忧，担心塑料会通过摄入较小碎片的鱼类和其他海洋生物而进入食物链。

13
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:32,360
Of course, the reason why there‘s so much plastic around is that it’s an amazingly useful material.
当然，有这么多塑料的原因是它是一种非常有用的材料。

14
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:33,920
We can‘t get enough of it.
我们对它的需求源源不断。

15
00:01:33,920 --> 00:01:39,040
It’s durable and adaptable, and is used for everything from yoghurt pots to spaceships.
它耐用且适应性强，从酸奶罐到宇宙飞船，各种东西都要用到它。

16
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:42,430
But it‘s precisely this quality which makes it a problem.
但恰好是这种特性使它成为了一个问题。

17
00:01:42,430 --> 00:01:48,600
The only way to destroy plastic is to heat or burn it - although this has the side effect of harmful emissions.
销毁塑料的唯一方法是加热或燃烧它——尽管这有产生有害排放物的副作用。

18
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:52,830
So what’s the alternative, other than using less plastic?
那么，除了减少使用塑料之外，还有什么其他办法呢？

19
00:01:52,830 --> 00:02:03,340
Oceanographer Dr Erik van Sebille from Utrecht University says we‘re facing a “tsunami” of plastic waste, and that the global waste industry needs to “get its act together”.
乌得勒支大学的海洋学家埃里克·范·塞比尔博士说，我们正面临着塑料垃圾的“海啸”，全球垃圾处理行业需要“行动起来”。

20
00:02:03,340 --> 00:02:09,520
Professor Richard Thompson, a marine biologist from Plymouth University, says it’s poor design that‘s at fault.
普利茅斯大学的海洋生物学家理查德·汤普森教授说，设计不佳难辞其咎。

21
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:16,540
He says that if products are currently designed “with recyclability in mind”, they could be recycled around 20 times over.
他说，如果目前的产品在设计时“考虑到可回收性”，它们可以被回收利用大约20次。

22
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:23,620
Dr Geyer agrees: “The holy grail of recycling is to keep material in use and in the loop for ever if you can.
盖耶博士表示赞同：“回收利用的圣杯就是要尽可能让材料一直被使用并处于循环之中。

23
00:02:23,620 --> 00:02:33,760
But it turns out in our study that actually 90% of that material that did get recycled - which I think we calculated was 600 million tonnes - only got recycled once.”
但我们的研究表明，在我们计算得出约6亿吨的回收材料中，实际上90%的材料只被回收利用了一次而已。”
